# C-type dipole yoke -- 1/1 FULL model (full geometry in all 3 axes)
#
# All three coordinate ranges are FULL (no domain reduction):
#   yoke x: [-12.5, +12.5] mm   (full pole thickness)
#   yoke y: [0, 182.5] mm       (gap-to-back-leg, no symmetry to apply)
#   yoke z: [-26.25, +26.25] mm (full pole / leg z-extent)
#   air sphere: full 4pi sr at the origin
#
# The Kelvin sphere is offset along +x (auto-Kelvin's default for the
# symmetry=["z"] case the auto-detect picks up here -- see below).
# No sym sidesets, no kelvin_far -- the Kelvin transformation alone
# handles the open boundary, with the GND vertex fixing the gauge.
# This variant is the reference baseline for the 1/2, 1/4, 1/8
# reductions: the same physics should give the SAME B-field at the
# origin (within mesh discretization), just at 2/4/8x the DOF cost.
#
# Mesh seam at z=0:
#   The air sphere is webcut at z=0 producing two hemispheres BOTH
#   retained in the mesh.  The webcut is a mesh seam ONLY (the
#   geometry is still a full sphere); it gives `add_kelvin_cubit`'s
#   copy-mesh step a 1:1 vertex correspondence between the air's
#   outer hemispheres and the Kelvin's webcut hemispheres.  Without
#   the seam the air's outer surface is a single closed sphere with
#   no curves, and copy-mesh has no source-curve / target-curve
#   anchors to align against. See the IH-style
#   validation_test/induction_heating/demoted_samples_legacy/
#   ih_fem_kelvin_sample.py for the same pattern.
#
# Coordinate system (matching em_1-2_half_z and em_sample_coil.py):
#   y = gap direction (pole tip at y~0, main leg at y~0.15)
#   z = beam direction
#   x = depth/thickness
#
# 3 yoke volumes, full extent in all three axes:
#   Vol 1: Pole piece  (x: [-12.5, +12.5], y: [0, 40],   z: [-25, +25] mm)
#   Vol 2: Yoke back   (x: [-12.5, +12.5], y: [40, 120], z: [-12.5, +12.5] mm)
#   Vol 3: Main leg    (x: [-12.5, +12.5], y: [120, 182.5], z: [-26.25, +26.25] mm)
#
# Use "Symmetry reduction" all OFF in the Export Mesh dialog (the
# default).  Auto-Kelvin auto-detects "no symmetry", places one
# full Kelvin sphere offset along +x, and copies the mesh from the
# air outer surface 1:1.
reset

# === Pole piece (near gap) ===
# Full: x [-12.5, +12.5], y [0, 40], z [-25, +25] mm
brick x 0.025 y 0.040 z 0.050
move volume 1 x 0 y 0.020 z 0

# === Yoke back (horizontal return) ===
# Full: x [-12.5, +12.5], y [40, 120], z [-12.5, +12.5] mm
brick x 0.025 y 0.080 z 0.025
move volume 2 x 0 y 0.080 z 0

# === Main leg (where coil sits) ===
# Full: x [-12.5, +12.5], y [120, 182.5], z [-26.25, +26.25] mm
brick x 0.025 y 0.0625 z 0.0525
move volume 3 x 0 y 0.15125 z 0

# === Imprint/merge for shared surfaces (continuous mesh) ===
imprint volume all
merge volume all

# === Hex mesh ===
volume all scheme map
volume 1 size 0.005
volume 2 size 0.008
volume 3 size 0.006
mesh volume all

# === Air sphere (full geometry + z=0 mesh seam) ===
create sphere radius 0.40
webcut volume 4 with plane zplane
# After webcut: vol 4 = z>0 hemisphere, vol 5 = z<0 hemisphere.  Both
# kept -- the seam is for Kelvin copy-mesh only, geometry is full.

# Subtract yoke from both hemispheres.
subtract volume 1 2 3 from volume 4 5 keep
imprint volume all
merge volume all
volume 4 5 scheme tetmesh
volume 4 5 size 0.02
mesh volume 4 5

# === Blocks ===
set duplicate block elements on
block 1 add volume 1 2 3
block 1 name "yoke"
block 2 add volume 4 5
block 2 name "air"

# Kelvin (FEM only): add via the launcher dialog "Add Kelvin open
# boundary (auto)" with all three "Symmetry reduction" combos set
# to OFF (default).  Auto-Kelvin auto-detects symmetry=["z"] from the
# z=0 webcut seam (multi-volume air, on z=0 plane, vertex range
# straddles 0 symmetrically), offsets a Kelvin sphere by +3R along x
# to (1.2, 0, 0), webcuts it at z=0, and copy-meshes air's
# hemispheres 1:1 onto the Kelvin's hemispheres.  GND nodeset at
# Kelvin centre anchors Omega=0.
